{
  "platform_id": "AODAwMANijw",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:AODAwMANijw",
    "purpose": "A recipe demonstration of baked ziti from The Food Lab. It uses soaked rather than boiled pasta, a split red and pink sauce, and cubed mozzarella, with food-science explanations along the way.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Soaking dried pasta in warm salted water instead of boiling it, then letting it cook in the oven, avoids the double-cooked, overcooked pasta of conventional baked ziti. It is also easier and needs no extra pot.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Dried pasta cooking has two separable stages: absorbing water (starch swelling) and heating (protein coagulation). Soaking handles the first, so the oven handles the second.",
      "Soak the pasta in warm salted water for about 20–30 minutes, stirring occasionally so higher-quality bronze-die pasta doesn't stick. It turns pale white when it has absorbed enough.",
      "The marinara is loosely based on Marcella Hazan's tomato sauce, using butter and olive oil to round out the tomatoes' flavor.",
      "Onions and garlic can be sweated together because the moisture from the onions protects the garlic from burning.",
      "Use crushed or whole peeled tomatoes, not diced; diced tomatoes contain calcium chloride and won't break down.",
      "Choose ricotta without stabilizers, and use cubed low-moisture full-fat mozzarella for gooey pockets.",
      "Half the sauce becomes a pink sauce with ricotta, cream, Parmesan, eggs and herbs, mixed with the pasta. The other half of the red sauce goes on top.",
      "Bake covered with foil at 400°F for 45 minutes, then uncovered about 15 minutes (optionally under the broiler). Finish with Parmesan, good olive oil and herbs, and ideally rest 10–15 minutes."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The science of pasta",
        "summary": "Introduces the Food Lab baked ziti and explains why boiled pasta overcooks in a casserole. Covers Ideas in Food's finding that hydration and cooking can be separated, and starts the pasta soaking.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing the sauce",
        "summary": "Sweats onion in butter and olive oil for a Marcella Hazan-inspired marinara and explains why onion and garlic can go in together. Adds chili flakes, oregano and basil stems, with tips on soaking pasta ahead and stirring it.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Sauce simmer and cheese prep",
        "summary": "Cubes low-moisture mozzarella and adds crushed tomatoes, explaining why to avoid diced tomatoes. Explains why cubed cheese beats shredded, with a digression on Chicago calling baked ziti 'lasagna'.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Mixing the filling",
        "summary": "Drains the soaked pasta and reserves half the red sauce. Turns the rest into a pink sauce with ricotta (avoiding stabilizers), Parmesan, eggs and herbs, then folds in the pasta and half the mozzarella.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 21
      },
      {
        "title": "Assembly and baking",
        "summary": "Pours the mixture into a deep baking dish and tops it with the reserved red sauce and mozzarella. Bakes it covered with foil at 400°F for 45 minutes, then uncovers it to brown.",
        "first_segment": 22,
        "last_segment": 24
      },
      {
        "title": "Finishing and tasting",
        "summary": "Broils briefly, then finishes with Parmesan, olive oil and herbs, and notes the value of resting. Tastes it, tallies the dairy and recaps the technique.",
        "first_segment": 25,
        "last_segment": 29
      }
    ],
